<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="bgi3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction</title>

<p> Water is essential for all life, yet its availability for human use is limited. 
    Although over 70% of Earth’s surface is covered by water, only a small fraction is freshwater—and an even smaller portion is readily accessible. 
    This freshwater is unevenly distributed across regions and populations.</p>
<p> In the United States, the average individual uses approximately 150 gallons of water per day, primarily for domestic purposes. 
    In contrast, many developing nations use significantly less. For example, in Ethiopia—a country with nearly twice the population of California—daily per capita water use is around 3 gallons.</p>
<p> Importantly, household consumption in the U.S. represents only about 13% of total water use. 
    The remaining 87% is consumed by agriculture and industry, highlighting the disproportionate demand placed on freshwater resources by large-scale human activities.</p>
<p> The hydrosphere encompasses all water on Earth—whether fresh or saline, in liquid, solid, or vapor form, and whether located on the surface, underground, or in the atmosphere. 
    Water continuously cycles through these reservoirs via the biogeochemical process known as the water cycle. 
    This cycle is driven by solar energy, which causes uneven heating and cooling of the atmosphere, landmasses, and oceans. 
    These temperature gradients generate wind, which, in combination with the Coriolis effect from Earth’s rotation, drives the movement of surface ocean currents. 
    Warm tropical waters flow toward the poles, carrying heat and moisture. 
    As this warm, moist air moves over cooler land, the moisture condenses and falls as precipitation.</p>
<p> Precipitation follows multiple pathways: it may run off into rivers and lakes, infiltrate surface soils to be absorbed by plants, or percolate deeper into underground aquifers. 
    Aquifers—subsurface reservoirs held within porous, permeable rock—account for approximately 30.1% of Earth’s freshwater. 
    While groundwater is present beneath most terrestrial regions, its accessibility varies. 
    In some areas, aquifers lie too deep for local communities to reach. 
    Recharge zones, such as wetlands, rivers, and lakes, allow water to filter into aquifers. 
    These systems are bounded by impermeable rock layers that define their size and flow characteristics. 
    Increasingly, aquifers are relied upon to supply water for rural and urban populations. 
    The soil and rock layers act as natural filtration systems, purifying water as it moves downward.</p>
<p> Surface water—including lakes, rivers, streams, ponds, and water in the atmosphere and living organisms—makes up only 1.2% of Earth’s freshwater. 
    Many metropolitan areas depend on reservoirs, which may be natural or human-made through damming rivers. 
    These reservoirs are vulnerable to depletion during droughts, as they rely on consistent rainfall for replenishment. 
    Additional threats to both surface and groundwater systems include pollution, excessive withdrawal, and the spread of impermeable surfaces that hinder recharge. 
    Water that is withdrawn for human use may be returned to the system, but water consumed by organisms or lost to evaporation is permanently removed from the cycle.</p>
<p> Oceans dominate the hydrosphere, containing 97% of Earth’s total water. 
    Although seawater is not directly usable for most human needs, oceans are ecologically vital. 
    They are major sources of oxygen and protein, and support productive fisheries, particularly in regions of deepwater upwelling. 
    These upwellings occur when nutrient-rich water from the ocean floor is brought to the surface by deep currents. 
    These currents are driven by thermohaline circulation—cold, dense water near the poles sinks and flows toward the equator, gradually warming and rising. 
    This movement creates a middle ocean layer that circulates back toward the poles, where it cools and sinks again. 
    When these currents encounter continental margins, they are deflected upward, enriching surface waters.</p>
<p> The remaining freshwater is stored in glaciers and polar ice caps, which hold approximately 68.7% of Earth’s freshwater. 
    Seasonal melting contributes to ocean water and influences deep ocean circulation. 
    However, glacial retreat is accelerating due to climate change. 
    During colder climatic periods, glaciers expand, trapping more freshwater and covering vast land areas. 
    One notable aquifer beneath the Great Plains of the United States contains water primarily derived from glacial melt during the last ice age.</p>
<p> The hydrosphere is a dynamic system of interconnected components, each influencing and responding to ecological and climatic processes. 
    Water moves through these systems at varying rates, and human activities increasingly affect its distribution, quality, and availability. 
    As ecological scientists, understanding these interactions is essential for predicting and managing the consequences of anthropogenic impacts on global water resources.</p>


</section>